          {
            "@context": "https://schema.org",
            "@type": "FAQPage",
            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is a PBM?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "A pharmacy benefit manager (PBM) is a third-party intermediary hired by a health plan to manage its prescription drug benefit. The Department of Labor's rule defines PBM services functionally, covering providers of pharmacy benefit management services regardless of whether the person identifies itself as a PBM."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does the DOL PBM fee disclosure rule lower my copays?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The rule does not directly lower worker copays. It imposes disclosure requirements on PBMs to report compensation to employer plan fiduciaries, but it contains no legal mandate setting or reducing copayments, coinsurance, or deductibles. Any change in worker costs depends on whether an employer renegotiates its contract after reviewing the fee disclosures."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "When does the DOL PBM fee disclosure rule take effect?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The regulation takes effect on January 4, 2027, and applies to plan years beginning on or after July 1, 2027. For standard calendar-year employer plans, the first covered plan year begins on January 1, 2028, by simple arithmetic on the Federal Register dates."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does the rule apply to my employer's drug plan?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The rule covers two kinds of group health plan. One is offered by a specified large employer, meaning an average of at least 100 employees, counted under IRC section 4980H aggregation rules. The other is a specified large plan, meaning an average of at least 100 participants. It excludes plans whose benefits are all provided through an insurance policy and plans of sponsors affiliated with drug manufacturers, distributors, wholesalers, PBMs or rebate aggregators. The Department describes the result as large, self-insured plans."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Will workers see the PBM disclosures?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No. The regulation requires PBMs to provide written disclosures to responsible employer plan fiduciaries who have the authority to contract for plan services. The operative text contains no requirement to distribute fee schedules, rebate amounts, or spread pricing data directly to plan participants."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is spread pricing?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The rule defines spread compensation as the difference, for each drug, between what the plan pays the PBM and what the PBM pays the pharmacy. PBMs must report it to plan fiduciaries by drug National Drug Code and pharmacy channel (retail, mail order and specialty)."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is a copay claw-back?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The regulation defines copay claw-back compensation as the difference between a copayment or coinsurance amount paid to the pharmacy by a plan participant and the reimbursement to the pharmacy. Under the rule, PBMs must disclose these recoupment amounts to plan fiduciaries."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is ERISA section 726?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "ERISA section 726 is a separate statutory provision added by the Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2026. It requires PBMs to give large self-insured plans semiannual drug spending reports, starting with plan years on or after August 3, 2028. It also requires a summary document that plans can give participants on request."
                }
              }
            ]
          },
